<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gloucester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Glevum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gloucester is a cathedral city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began as the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress and colonia of Glevum, fell to the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> after 577 and became a centre of the Hwicce and of Mercia. Its abbey, now the cathedral, holds the tomb of Edward II, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday survey</a> there in 1085.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gloucester.jpg" alt="Gloucester" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gloucester_Docks_at_Night.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ceawlin, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by Bede among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the Chronicle records his victory at the Battle of Dyrham in 577, which brought <b>Gloucester</b>, Cirencester and Bath under West Saxon control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people lived in farmsteads and villages much like those of the Iron Age, but a network of towns grew up: coloniae of veterans at Colchester, Lincoln and <b>Gloucester</b>, later York, and civitas capitals for the native peoples, such as Verulamium (St Albans), Corinium (Cirencester) and Viroconium (Wroxeter).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Howells said he wrote it after watching railway trucks shunting on the line below a cottage window in <b>Gloucester</b>, and dedicated it to his mother; its final cadence is much admired, and his fellow composer Patrick Hadley reportedly told him he would like to die to it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>Brackett lived at the Sabbathday Lake Shaker Village in New <b>Gloucester</b> before he was called to serve in the Ministry in 1848; for the next decade he served the community at Alfred, and later returned to New Gloucester.</blockquote><a href="/songs/simple-gifts">Simple Gifts</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard, Duke of <b>Gloucester</b>, was crowned after his nephew Edward V was declared illegitimate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Rout of Winchester, where Robert of <b>Gloucester</b>, chief supporter of Empress Matilda, was captured, the two sides exchanged him for King Stephen, whom Matilda's forces had held.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
